    "description": "Technical lead of a multi-tenant ERP for equipment dealers (sales, service, rentals, inventory, accounting), serving ~200 dealers in North America and Australia.\n\nSystems\n• Inventory & costing: leading the overhaul of the inventory ledger behind every dealer's books; turned a nightly audit into a specification of correct behavior and fixed root causes (double-posting, race conditions, costing errors), cutting new discrepancies by more than half in the first month.\n• AI agent platform: built a Kubernetes platform where AI agents investigate audit cases overnight in a credential-free sandbox and draft root causes and fixes for human review, with an evaluation harness, review console and MCP servers.\n• Customer-facing MCP service: leading a new service for AI-driven reporting over dealers' own data.\n• GraphQL backend: created the domain-driven GraphQL backend that became the team's primary API, with code generation for typed data loaders; also built the permissions service and a public API.\n• Rental system: architected the rental platform (contracts, interim billing, kits, rent-to-own) used by over 100 dealers.\n• Machine sales & purchasing: built the machine purchase-order system (ordering, approvals, receiving, costing).\n• Accounting: led the QuickBooks App Store security review, built a Sage Intacct integration, designed the QuickBooks Online, tax and multi-currency architecture.\n• Integrations: OEM EDI, payments, webhooks, Elasticsearch, Auth0 OIDC.\n\nTeam\n• Led the Alaska team from 2019; on the management team since 2022.\n• Moved to Boston to build a new engineering office; mentored 12+ engineers.\n• Run engineering hiring; involved in hiring most of the ~22-person engineering and data team.\n• Introduced agile practices and reorganized engineering into domain-owning product squads.\n• Wrote the incident response plan and led security remediation work.\n• Built the data-migration program for dealer onboardings from major competing systems.",
